00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:10.430
So what is Cloud Spanner?

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:14.690
So Cloud Spanner is GCP,
Google Cloud Platform's,

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:16.550
enterprise-grade,
globally-distributed,

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:20.010
and strongly-consistent
distributed database system.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:21.980
And it's built
specifically for the cloud

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:24.470
to combine the benefits of
both relational database

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:30.330
structure and non-relational
horizontal scale.

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:31.890
Now what did that mean?

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:36.060
So essentially, we've combined
the relational semantics

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.940
of schemas, ACID
transactions, and SQL

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:41.730
as well as the horizontal
scale and availability

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:45.450
of NoSQL systems into
one fully managed

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:48.295
and easy-to-use relational
database service.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.540
What that means is
you don't have to do

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.910
any more trade-offs or tuning.

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:58.540
So basically, you don't have
to make the decision of,

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:03.320
do I abandon relational
semantics to get scale,

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:06.920
or do I abandon scale to get the
relational semantics because I

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:10.699
can't actually do without
SQL and transactions

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:13.700
across rows and tables?

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.800
With Spanner, you
get the best of both.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:19.910
So you can basically
have higher availability.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:23.920
We actually offer 99.999%,
five nines of availability SLA,

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:25.640
for our multi-region
configuration.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:29.150
And all of this is packaged up
into one easy-to-use system.

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:35.010
So in terms of
management, because we

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:38.117
are a fully managed
system, what that means is

00:02:38.117 --> 00:02:40.200
you don't have to have the
burden and the overhead

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.930
of constantly worrying about
keeping your database up

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:44.670
and running.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:46.516
In fact, it takes
four clicks for you

00:02:46.516 --> 00:02:49.950
to even spin up a
globally-distributed database.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:51.300
You can go to our Cloud Console.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:54.354
You can just create an
instance, create a database,

00:02:54.354 --> 00:02:55.020
create a schema.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:56.230
And you're good to go.

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:58.960
So what that means is you
don't have to worry about,

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:00.809
oh my gosh, if I have
to create an index,

00:03:00.809 --> 00:03:02.350
do I have to bring
down the database?

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:03.480
If I have to add
a table, do I have

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:04.710
to bring down the database?

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:08.070
There isn't any management
or maintenance windows

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:10.260
that you have to worry
about because all of that

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:15.210
is taken care of by us and
our fantastic [INAUDIBLE]

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:16.990
team in the background.

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:19.150
And you just worry
about your database

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:20.500
and getting the most out of it.

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:26.260
Because we combine
relational semantics

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:28.930
with scale, what that
means is that you

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:30.040
get to launch apps faster.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:31.690
Because you don't
have to ever worry

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:33.430
about learning a new language.

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:36.430
We have ANSI, 2011
compliant SQL.

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:38.980
We have schemas, just like
any other relational database

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:40.020
that you're used to.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:43.960
We have security and
high availability

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:47.110
built into the system so
you don't have to-- they're

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:50.910
not nickel-and-dime add-ons
that you have to pay for extra.

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:52.840
They just come part
of the package.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:55.870
And all of that kind
of gets offered to you

00:03:55.870 --> 00:04:01.750
so that you and your developers
can create the best database

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:04.436
that you want on
top of our solution.

00:04:04.436 --> 00:04:07.260
All of this, by the
way, is given to you

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:08.700
with strong consistency.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:11.130
Cloud Spanner is always
strongly consistent.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:13.590
There isn't any,
which consistency

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:17.579
do I need to choose to get
the best out of my system?

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:19.584
You always get strongly
consistent data,

00:04:19.584 --> 00:04:21.459
which means you don't
have to worry about it.

00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:25.650
You don't have to do cross
shard transactions, any of that.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:27.660
Because it's scalable,
you get all of that

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.110
built into the system.

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:30.810
And you never have
to worry about

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:34.050
inconsistent, incorrect,
inaccurate data, which

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:36.960
means you can just build
your application on top

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.450
of your strongly consistent
Cloud Spanner database.

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:41.190
And you can just go.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:46.740
You never have to think about
replatforming or really crazy

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:49.650
bugs because of
consistency semantics

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:51.150
that are not met
or are not the way

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:53.490
you expected them to
be off your data--

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:56.400
which means you're always,
always getting consistent data

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:01.474
and always able to
create the best database,

00:05:01.474 --> 00:05:04.140
get the best database semantics,
and build the best applications

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:05.050
as fast as you want.

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:06.600
And you can just launch and go.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:11.990
So as I said earlier,
today's challenge

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:14.800
is to ask me anything
about Cloud Spanner.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:18.590
And let's have a discussion
about distributed systems

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:21.200
and how we made Cloud Spanner
possible and available

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:23.810
to all of you out there.

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:26.280
Stay tuned for the live Q&amp;A.
We'll be back in less than a

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:26.780
minute.

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:40.870
Welcome back.

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:43.570
And let's get
started with our Q&amp;A.

00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:45.960
So the first
question we have is,

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:48.310
is Cloud Spanner a
drop-in replacement

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:50.620
for other relational databases?

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:52.150
So it is a relational database.

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:52.910
Cloud Spanner is.

00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:55.000
But it's also
horizontally scalable.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:58.150
What that means is you
can use your schemas.

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:00.430
Especially for simple
relational schemas,

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:02.770
it should be a
complete transformation

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:04.070
back into Cloud Spanner.

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:05.110
We have DDL statements.

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:06.320
We have joins.

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:08.600
We have all that stuff.

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:11.360
So it shouldn't be a
problem to translate.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:13.510
However-- and this is
true if you've ever

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:15.550
used a relational
database-- each database has

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:16.750
its own quirks.

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:19.750
And each database requires
some massaging, especially

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:21.970
for the more complicated
datasets and the more

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:23.230
complicated data types.

00:07:23.230 --> 00:07:27.670
So in general, I don't
anticipate any problem

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:29.980
with taking your
relational database

00:07:29.980 --> 00:07:31.450
and moving it to Spanner.

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:35.990
But there's always caveats
depending on the dataset

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:36.805
and the data type.

00:07:36.805 --> 00:07:38.680
The one interesting
thing about Cloud Spanner

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:42.550
is that because it offers what
we call interleave statements,

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:46.510
which is essentially a
pre-join of your tables,

00:07:46.510 --> 00:07:48.490
it actually allows
you to structure

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:53.230
within a relational database
hierarchical datasets.

00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:55.900
And you can actually
pre-join two tables

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:57.880
by putting interleave
clauses in.

00:07:57.880 --> 00:07:59.800
So you can do fast scans.

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:01.480
So you can think
about it as instead

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:05.080
of having to do a join
at every query time,

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:07.840
for data that is queried
together very often,

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:10.580
you can actually
interleave and join.

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:13.660
Predetermined, the
dataset is joined.

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:18.490
And then Cloud Spanner can take
that as a hint to quickly scan

00:08:18.490 --> 00:08:19.910
over a bunch of data.

00:08:19.910 --> 00:08:21.610
So hopefully that answered that.

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:23.710
The next question we
have is, how do you

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:27.860
guarantee four nines and
five nines of availability?

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:28.950
That's a great question.

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:32.120
So just to recap, Cloud
Spanner offers four nines

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:35.289
of availability for
our regional instances

00:08:35.289 --> 00:08:39.799
and five nines for our
multi-region instances.

00:08:39.799 --> 00:08:43.940
So if you look at
GCP, GCP zoned itself

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:47.270
by themselves offer three
nines of availability per zone.

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:49.640
And as you may
know, Cloud Spanner

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:54.800
is replicated within three
zones for the regional instance

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:59.900
and across three regions
and at least five zones

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:02.490
for the multi-region
configurations.

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:04.250
And multi-region
configurations differ,

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:07.020
so it could be all the
way up to nine zones.

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:10.190
So the idea is if you really
look at the probabilities,

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:12.830
even for the regional
instance, if you multiply out

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:16.580
the probabilities for 99.99%
across three zones, that's

00:09:16.580 --> 00:09:18.290
a lot of nines.

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:21.320
But we know that all
failures aren't independent.

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:25.100
Because, for example,
within a region,

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:27.530
there is some
geographic correlations,

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:30.350
like if a meteor strikes
or something like that.

00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:34.460
So keeping that realistic,
we offer the four nines

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:35.390
for the regional.

00:09:35.390 --> 00:09:39.110
And in the multi-region case,
because we have actually

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:42.360
distributed the database across
multiple regions-- as I said,

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:43.370
at least three--

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:47.270
that means we are taking
care of not having

00:09:47.270 --> 00:09:49.610
geographic correlated faults.

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:56.720
So in that case, we can
remove one element of risk.

00:09:56.720 --> 00:09:59.572
And we can offer you
up to five nines.

00:09:59.572 --> 00:10:02.070
I hope that made sense.

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:07.432
Our next question is, I read
about interleaved tables.

00:10:07.432 --> 00:10:08.640
What's the advantage of that?

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:12.180
So I think I mentioned that in
the first question actually.

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:17.610
But basically, what the
interleave statement in our DDL

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:21.030
language is is a hint
to Spanner to say

00:10:21.030 --> 00:10:27.330
that you will query these two
datasets very often together.

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:30.540
So if you have a-- let's
say you have a table albums

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:31.950
and another table singers.

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:35.310
And because you're running,
let's say, a music service,

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:42.410
for each user, you have multiple
albums and multiple singers.

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:46.920
So you constantly query for
a singer all their albums--

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:50.040
which means, in a
traditional sense,

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:53.250
you will have to do a
join of singers and albums

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:54.790
and then produce the results.

00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:59.580
In Spanner's case, if you say
interleave albums and singers,

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:02.780
what that means is that
the system, Spanner itself,

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:06.390
will pre-join the
dataset and store it--

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:09.300
such that when you actually
query them together,

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:12.060
it'll just do a fast scan,
which means you avoid

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:15.640
the penalty of [AUDIO OUT]

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:18.910
So our next question is, if
I start with a single region,

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:22.510
is it possible to scale
to multi-region easily?

00:11:22.510 --> 00:11:25.300
So currently, the way we
expect customers to do it

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:30.550
is to take an export of
their Cloud Spanner database

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:35.710
and then import it into a
multi-region configuration.

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:37.360
We hope that in
the future, we'll

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:41.540
have an easy solution for you
to have this as a managed thing

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:43.150
so you can just
tell Spanner, hey,

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:45.934
I want to move this database
from this single region

00:11:45.934 --> 00:11:47.350
into a multi-region
configuration.

00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:49.414
And you can specify both.

00:11:49.414 --> 00:11:50.830
And you should be
able to do that.

00:11:50.830 --> 00:11:54.490
But that's not
currently what we offer.

00:11:54.490 --> 00:11:56.650
So our next question
is, is there

00:11:56.650 --> 00:12:00.400
a documented migration path
from Cloud SQL to Cloud Spanner?

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:02.740
So there isn't a
documented path.

00:12:02.740 --> 00:12:06.160
But we do have customer use
cases of how they've done it.

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:13.480
So Quizlet, for example, is an
online test study application.

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:17.680
And they've actually migrated
from their multiple--

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:19.300
I can't say how
many, but multiple--

00:12:19.300 --> 00:12:22.300
Cloud SQL shards into one
Cloud Spanner database.

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:26.380
And they've written a case study
on it that you can find online.

00:12:26.380 --> 00:12:28.711
It's linked from
our docs actually.

00:12:28.711 --> 00:12:29.710
So you can look at that.

00:12:29.710 --> 00:12:32.670
We have other such use
cases and stories as well.

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:36.760
Again, we hope to have
solutions papers for customers

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:37.780
to be able to do this.

00:12:37.780 --> 00:12:39.670
And they're coming shortly.

00:12:39.670 --> 00:12:41.350
But as of right
now, you should be

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:45.800
able to find information based
on the customer case studies.

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:50.050
Next, when would you choose
Cloud Spanner over Cloud SQL?

00:12:50.050 --> 00:12:51.470
That's an interesting question.

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:54.080
So there are obviously
two use cases.

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:57.730
One is if you've already
been using Cloud SQL,

00:12:57.730 --> 00:13:00.940
and you've had to go to the
sharded solution for Cloud SQL

00:13:00.940 --> 00:13:03.460
because your dataset grew.

00:13:03.460 --> 00:13:06.640
Or your application
got really successful,

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:10.000
and you have more
QPS, more queries,

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:13.570
coming in that your one
box Cloud SQL or even

00:13:13.570 --> 00:13:16.120
any MySQL system can offer.

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:19.180
In that case, Cloud Spanner
is an obvious choice

00:13:19.180 --> 00:13:22.750
because you don't have to
give up your wonderful SQL

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:24.840
and relational semantics
to be able to deal

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:26.590
with the scale, which
is what you would've

00:13:26.590 --> 00:13:30.580
had to do if you chose a
scalable solution, which

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:32.550
was NoSQL.

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:35.610
And so you can just take
your Cloud SQL shards,

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:37.650
and then migrate over
to Cloud Spanner,

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:40.650
and then never have to worry
about replatforming again.

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:43.020
In terms of if you're starting
out with a new Greenfield

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:46.020
application, again,
if you expect

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:49.540
to be growing very quickly
either in terms of dataset

00:13:49.540 --> 00:13:57.000
or app QPS or a combination, if
you start with Cloud Spanner,

00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:58.800
you never have to replatform.

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:02.040
So you have the ability to
build this system once, and then

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:05.880
keep it growing, and never
have to worry about maintenance

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:08.910
and the hazards of,
my box fell over

00:14:08.910 --> 00:14:12.120
because it ran out of memory,
and all those kinds of things.

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:14.380
However, if you're
comfortable with Cloud SQL,

00:14:14.380 --> 00:14:18.240
and you don't think you need
any of the high availability,

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:23.880
or management, or scalability,
or relational semantics

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:25.080
of Cloud Spanner--

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:26.820
then if you're happy
with Cloud SQL--

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:28.620
you've already been building
the applications on it.

00:14:28.620 --> 00:14:31.000
You don't expect the dataset
to grow or the QPS to grow.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:33.250
And as I said, you don't
expect to be-- you've already

00:14:33.250 --> 00:14:36.131
got all the maintenance pieces
in place and management pieces

00:14:36.131 --> 00:14:36.630
in place.

00:14:36.630 --> 00:14:39.480
Then by all means, if
you're happy with Cloud SQL,

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:40.590
please continue to use it.

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:47.860
Next, can I explain how
Cloud Spanner replicas

00:14:47.860 --> 00:14:49.780
work in the background?

00:14:49.780 --> 00:14:52.670
Does Spanner use
synchronous replication?

00:14:52.670 --> 00:14:55.300
Yes, it does use
synchronous replication.

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:59.080
And I have a few slides
here that can basically

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:00.220
walk us through this.

00:15:00.220 --> 00:15:04.090
But basically, as I
mentioned, Cloud Spanner

00:15:04.090 --> 00:15:07.090
is synchronously replicated,
under the covers.

00:15:07.090 --> 00:15:13.990
So you will only ever have to
deal with one database and one

00:15:13.990 --> 00:15:14.890
database endpoint.

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:17.110
You never have to worry
about which replica

00:15:17.110 --> 00:15:20.350
you need to talk to or rebalance
all replica-- none of that.

00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:24.760
That's just completely
hidden from you.

00:15:24.760 --> 00:15:28.100
You have to just worry about
talking to your database.

00:15:28.100 --> 00:15:30.170
So we have a few
slides here, as I said.

00:15:30.170 --> 00:15:32.360
Let's talk about the
regional configuration

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:36.580
first because it's the
simplest to talk about.

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:40.240
So as you can see here, we've
taken one regional Cloud

00:15:40.240 --> 00:15:42.210
Spanner instance, which
has three replicas,

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:44.000
as I mentioned earlier.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:47.640
These three replicas are
in three different GCP

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:49.360
zones within a given region.

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:51.490
We've chosen a European
region, in this case.

00:15:51.490 --> 00:15:54.520
But it could be any GCP region.

00:15:54.520 --> 00:15:58.350
And as you can see, there are
three read/write replicas all

00:15:58.350 --> 00:16:00.240
off the database
within this region.

00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:06.370
In the multi-region case,
things get a little bit more

00:16:06.370 --> 00:16:06.870
complicated.

00:16:06.870 --> 00:16:09.340
But the original idea
remains the same,

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:13.530
which is that you
have multiple replicas

00:16:13.530 --> 00:16:17.130
across multiple
different GCP regions

00:16:17.130 --> 00:16:20.220
across multiple different GCP
zones within those regions.

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:22.920
So we have three
regions in the US

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:24.795
in this particular
configuration.

00:16:24.795 --> 00:16:26.670
This is one of the
configurations we actually

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:27.170
offer.

00:16:27.170 --> 00:16:32.280
It's our [? nam ur Asia 1 ?]
region, which is basically

00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:35.140
a global configuration.

00:16:35.140 --> 00:16:38.370
So what that means is
we have the write quorum

00:16:38.370 --> 00:16:42.390
in the US split across,
or spread across,

00:16:42.390 --> 00:16:53.070
three different US GCP regions,
and an Asia region with two

00:16:53.070 --> 00:16:56.460
Asia regions each having
read-only replicas,

00:16:56.460 --> 00:17:00.930
and then a European set
which has two replicas

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:03.460
both in different GCP regions.

00:17:03.460 --> 00:17:05.910
So what that means
is we've basically

00:17:05.910 --> 00:17:10.170
distributed the data across
different geographic locations

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:14.880
to reduce the probability
of correlated faults

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:18.276
due to natural or
geographic issues.

00:17:18.276 --> 00:17:19.650
So as you can see
here, there are

00:17:19.650 --> 00:17:25.710
nine replicas spread across
seven different GCP regions.

00:17:28.450 --> 00:17:31.720
Now, what does that
mean in terms of how you

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:33.400
interact with Cloud Spanner?

00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:35.960
So you have one Cloud
Spanner instance

00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:39.220
which has all these
replicas of the databases.

00:17:39.220 --> 00:17:41.800
In that instance, you can
have more than one database

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:46.010
if you want in an instance,
depending on your use case.

00:17:46.010 --> 00:17:49.820
And each of these databases
have multiple tables.

00:17:49.820 --> 00:17:54.600
And they go to multiple
splits of the database.

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:56.560
So what that means is because--

00:17:56.560 --> 00:17:59.420
so Cloud Spanner is a sharded,
synchronously-replicated

00:17:59.420 --> 00:18:00.540
database.

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:03.920
And each database
actually has a full copy

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:07.130
of the data across the
multiple replicas, which

00:18:07.130 --> 00:18:09.440
means all the tables
and all the data

00:18:09.440 --> 00:18:13.130
is stored in all the
different regions fully.

00:18:13.130 --> 00:18:16.250
And the data is
split across multiple

00:18:16.250 --> 00:18:19.970
what we call splits
to allow it to spread

00:18:19.970 --> 00:18:23.030
over multiple different
nodes, Cloud Spanner

00:18:23.030 --> 00:18:26.959
nodes, to again reduce the
chances of a single failure

00:18:26.959 --> 00:18:28.250
bringing down the whole system.

00:18:28.250 --> 00:18:29.810
So we're parallelizing
and splitting

00:18:29.810 --> 00:18:33.218
and sharding, all of which
is happening automatically.

00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:40.180
So as we talked about,
there are multiple zones.

00:18:40.180 --> 00:18:43.090
And if an update
comes in, basically we

00:18:43.090 --> 00:18:47.560
use Paxos to replicate
the data and get consensus

00:18:47.560 --> 00:18:49.870
into committing a write.

00:18:49.870 --> 00:18:51.510
So in this case,
there's an update

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:53.260
that comes in for table
one and table two.

00:18:53.260 --> 00:18:56.770
Let's say you want to do an
insert across both tables.

00:18:56.770 --> 00:18:58.750
And then the Paxos
protocol we have,

00:18:58.750 --> 00:19:02.860
which is by the way the
protocol that we wrote ourselves

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:05.150
to optimize-- it's not the
open-source Paxos protocol.

00:19:05.150 --> 00:19:07.610
We have our own
optimized version of it

00:19:07.610 --> 00:19:10.930
to again give us the performance
and high availability

00:19:10.930 --> 00:19:13.960
that we've talked about so much.

00:19:13.960 --> 00:19:15.380
So basically, an
update comes in.

00:19:15.380 --> 00:19:19.870
We use Paxos to commit the
write across all replicas.

00:19:19.870 --> 00:19:24.130
And by the time you get
the acknowledgment back

00:19:24.130 --> 00:19:26.050
of the write-- so if
the commit succeeds,

00:19:26.050 --> 00:19:29.380
you are guaranteed that the
data has been persisted.

00:19:29.380 --> 00:19:34.420
And you can query it as soon
as you get the commit back.

00:19:34.420 --> 00:19:39.010
So let's move on to
other questions here.

00:19:39.010 --> 00:19:40.000
Hopefully, that helped.

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:44.350
And I can go into it in more
detail if folks are interested.

00:19:44.350 --> 00:19:49.640
So going back to our question
set, is there a doc-- sorry,

00:19:49.640 --> 00:19:50.920
I lost my train of thought.

00:19:53.810 --> 00:19:55.820
How does Cloud Spanner
ensure that there isn't

00:19:55.820 --> 00:19:57.420
a single point of failure?

00:19:57.420 --> 00:20:00.420
So I think I covered some
of this in our discussion

00:20:00.420 --> 00:20:05.180
so far, which is that Cloud
Spanner's both replicated

00:20:05.180 --> 00:20:09.260
across multiple zones
and multiple GCP regions.

00:20:09.260 --> 00:20:12.560
It's also sharded within a zone.

00:20:12.560 --> 00:20:16.670
So a zone is where a replica, a
full copy of the Cloud Spanner

00:20:16.670 --> 00:20:18.350
database, exists.

00:20:18.350 --> 00:20:21.650
And now it's not one
monolithic structure.

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:22.670
It's sharded.

00:20:22.670 --> 00:20:27.170
And each shard is put on
one of the multiple nodes

00:20:27.170 --> 00:20:30.920
that you assigned
for Cloud Spanner.

00:20:30.920 --> 00:20:36.146
So because of the sharding and
because of the replication,

00:20:36.146 --> 00:20:37.520
we can guarantee
that there isn't

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:38.700
a single point of failure.

00:20:38.700 --> 00:20:40.580
Because even if
one node goes down,

00:20:40.580 --> 00:20:42.290
which I'll get into in a
minute of what happens in case

00:20:42.290 --> 00:20:44.498
of failure-- but even if
one node goes down and there

00:20:44.498 --> 00:20:47.180
are multiple nodes,
the other nodes

00:20:47.180 --> 00:20:50.370
are up and running
to serve data.

00:20:50.370 --> 00:20:53.420
Now I'm talking about one
of the multiple zones.

00:20:53.420 --> 00:20:57.150
So in one zone, if you have
10 nodes and one goes down,

00:20:57.150 --> 00:21:00.800
then at most that one tenth--
at most, one tenth of data

00:21:00.800 --> 00:21:02.730
is supposed to be unavailable.

00:21:02.730 --> 00:21:07.100
But because we are also
replicated across zones,

00:21:07.100 --> 00:21:10.190
if the client notices that
one node that it was talking

00:21:10.190 --> 00:21:13.730
to goes down, it will actually
just contact the other regions

00:21:13.730 --> 00:21:14.990
with the same data.

00:21:14.990 --> 00:21:18.710
So from the client or
the customer viewpoint,

00:21:18.710 --> 00:21:21.290
you actually don't
even notice this--

00:21:21.290 --> 00:21:23.450
on top of which,
we're constantly

00:21:23.450 --> 00:21:24.980
monitoring the
health of our nodes

00:21:24.980 --> 00:21:28.190
and of the system overall,
all different aspects of it.

00:21:28.190 --> 00:21:32.390
And so we're constantly
rebalancing and resharding

00:21:32.390 --> 00:21:35.240
these nodes and
the data on them.

00:21:35.240 --> 00:21:40.700
So in fact, the failure scenario
isn't really a huge deal

00:21:40.700 --> 00:21:43.910
because we are constantly
already automatically changing

00:21:43.910 --> 00:21:46.100
the dataset, and
where it resides,

00:21:46.100 --> 00:21:47.730
and which nodes it sits on.

00:21:47.730 --> 00:21:51.620
So it's not a one-time event
where something happened,

00:21:51.620 --> 00:21:54.440
and now you have a
huge outage or a blip

00:21:54.440 --> 00:21:58.340
because the one or
two nodes fell down.

00:21:58.340 --> 00:22:02.450
The whole thing is just
automatically managed for you.

00:22:02.450 --> 00:22:06.710
So you, as a Cloud Spanner user,
don't even notice in most cases

00:22:06.710 --> 00:22:09.500
that one or two nodes went down.

00:22:09.500 --> 00:22:11.000
This is actually
one of the reasons

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:14.810
why we say that the SLA
is offered for at least

00:22:14.810 --> 00:22:16.160
three nodes in an instance.

00:22:16.160 --> 00:22:19.340
Because, as you can see,
if you have only one

00:22:19.340 --> 00:22:21.890
node and that node went
down, then, yes, your data

00:22:21.890 --> 00:22:25.120
is at risk, even though
there are two other replicas

00:22:25.120 --> 00:22:26.380
that you can talk to.

00:22:26.380 --> 00:22:29.740
But in the case of three nodes
that you get for a production

00:22:29.740 --> 00:22:33.280
instance, there are
actually three times three

00:22:33.280 --> 00:22:35.140
in the regional
case, so nine nodes,

00:22:35.140 --> 00:22:38.450
that can serve your data
at any given point in time.

00:22:38.450 --> 00:22:43.900
So that and a bunch of other
optimizations that we've made,

00:22:43.900 --> 00:22:47.950
including the GCP network
and the full GCP stack,

00:22:47.950 --> 00:22:49.984
allow us to have the no
single point of failure.

00:22:49.984 --> 00:22:52.150
Because there are a bunch
of virtualized components,

00:22:52.150 --> 00:22:58.390
all of which are constantly
rebalancing and healing.

00:22:58.390 --> 00:23:02.287
Moving on to our next
question from the audience,

00:23:02.287 --> 00:23:04.120
when would I consider
Spanner over BigTable?

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:06.430
Great question.

00:23:06.430 --> 00:23:09.034
So Spanner is a
relational database.

00:23:09.034 --> 00:23:10.450
I think that's the
first thing you

00:23:10.450 --> 00:23:15.130
need to think about
when you're choosing

00:23:15.130 --> 00:23:20.330
which data storage system you
need to use for your dataset.

00:23:20.330 --> 00:23:25.370
Do you want a relational
database with SQL,

00:23:25.370 --> 00:23:27.790
with schemas, with
high availability,

00:23:27.790 --> 00:23:33.360
with cross globally available
distributed database?

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:35.510
Or do you not need that?

00:23:35.510 --> 00:23:39.730
BigTable is great for
the scalability aspect

00:23:39.730 --> 00:23:43.160
and for doing a bunch
of high-throughput reads

00:23:43.160 --> 00:23:43.820
and writes--

00:23:43.820 --> 00:23:46.880
such as if you have
[INAUDIBLE] et cetera,

00:23:46.880 --> 00:23:49.790
there's a lot of information
on BigTable use cases.

00:23:49.790 --> 00:23:52.370
But the point is it's
not a SQL database.

00:23:52.370 --> 00:23:53.630
It's a NoSQL database.

00:23:53.630 --> 00:23:56.330
It's horizontally scalable.

00:23:56.330 --> 00:23:59.270
And it has a lot of
great properties.

00:23:59.270 --> 00:24:03.980
But it doesn't offer you DDL, or
cross-role, cross-region, ACID

00:24:03.980 --> 00:24:04.670
transactions.

00:24:04.670 --> 00:24:06.960
It doesn't offer you SQL.

00:24:06.960 --> 00:24:12.950
And it doesn't offer you
schemas with DDL statements.

00:24:12.950 --> 00:24:18.330
The next audience question, how
many nodes can Spanner grow to?

00:24:18.330 --> 00:24:20.130
How many nodes can you buy?

00:24:20.130 --> 00:24:23.250
So Spanner is--

00:24:23.250 --> 00:24:25.395
I don't want to use the
word infinitely scalable.

00:24:25.395 --> 00:24:28.540
But it's designed to
be scalable seriously

00:24:28.540 --> 00:24:30.780
to petabytes of
data and hundreds

00:24:30.780 --> 00:24:33.090
upon hundreds of nodes.

00:24:33.090 --> 00:24:37.170
So really, the real
question is, what

00:24:37.170 --> 00:24:43.220
is the capacity of the cluster
or the datacenter that you

00:24:43.220 --> 00:24:46.080
are buying Spanner in,
really at some level?

00:24:46.080 --> 00:24:50.970
And how much money do you want
to spend to buy Spanner nodes?

00:24:50.970 --> 00:24:54.510
But the technology
inherently is meant

00:24:54.510 --> 00:24:57.270
to be scalable while providing
ACID transactions, which

00:24:57.270 --> 00:25:00.960
is what makes it so cool and
why I'm so excited about it.

00:25:00.960 --> 00:25:03.390
And I can tell you from
our internal use cases

00:25:03.390 --> 00:25:05.970
that scaling to
petabytes of data

00:25:05.970 --> 00:25:10.420
and the associated
compute power required

00:25:10.420 --> 00:25:13.090
is really not a problem.

00:25:13.090 --> 00:25:16.386
Next question, I was
wondering how long

00:25:16.386 --> 00:25:17.510
this would take to come up.

00:25:17.510 --> 00:25:19.770
Does Cloud Spanner
break the CAP theorem?

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:21.990
Absolutely not.

00:25:21.990 --> 00:25:24.810
So the CAP theorem is about
maintaining availability

00:25:24.810 --> 00:25:28.770
in the face of partition
and maintaining consistency

00:25:28.770 --> 00:25:30.000
in the face of partitions.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:35.220
And the way Cloud Spanner is
able to achieve availability

00:25:35.220 --> 00:25:38.910
while having
partitions and achieve

00:25:38.910 --> 00:25:40.940
strong consistency in
the face of partitions

00:25:40.940 --> 00:25:43.300
is because twofold.

00:25:43.300 --> 00:25:47.000
One, the entire
stack is designed

00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:49.760
to have not a single point of
failure, as we talked about.

00:25:49.760 --> 00:25:54.710
So it's got sharding,
replication, synchronous

00:25:54.710 --> 00:25:56.717
replication, and
sharding built in.

00:25:56.717 --> 00:25:58.550
And we, again, use the
Paxos protocol, which

00:25:58.550 --> 00:26:00.190
means that we don't have to--

00:26:00.190 --> 00:26:02.930
as long as we have a majority,
we can continue to write.

00:26:02.930 --> 00:26:05.180
And as long as we have at
least one replica available,

00:26:05.180 --> 00:26:07.221
we can actually continue
to read for a long time.

00:26:09.860 --> 00:26:14.020
There's a really great paper
written by Eric Brewer--

00:26:14.020 --> 00:26:16.940
who's the author
of the CAP theorem,

00:26:16.940 --> 00:26:19.820
who's talked about CAP theorem
and Spanner and what that

00:26:19.820 --> 00:26:20.840
means--

00:26:20.840 --> 00:26:24.380
that I encourage anybody who's
interested to go read it.

00:26:24.380 --> 00:26:27.020
It describes all of these
things in much more detail.

00:26:27.020 --> 00:26:31.230
It's on our website,
cloud.google.com/spanner.

00:26:31.230 --> 00:26:34.910
And to round out that thought,
the other reason we can do this

00:26:34.910 --> 00:26:37.430
is because of
Google's networking.

00:26:37.430 --> 00:26:43.280
So we have put a lot of thought
and careful instrumentation

00:26:43.280 --> 00:26:45.770
of our networks to
make sure that we

00:26:45.770 --> 00:26:50.150
can continue to operate across
multiple types of network

00:26:50.150 --> 00:26:51.090
failures.

00:26:51.090 --> 00:26:54.279
And we have a bunch of
redundancy and, as I said,

00:26:54.279 --> 00:26:55.820
health checking and
health monitoring

00:26:55.820 --> 00:27:00.180
built in to reroute traffic if
we notice a slowdown somewhere.

00:27:00.180 --> 00:27:03.324
Or if we notice some
paths are blocked,

00:27:03.324 --> 00:27:04.490
then we can kind of reroute.

00:27:04.490 --> 00:27:06.770
All of this happening
automatically,

00:27:06.770 --> 00:27:09.770
and the system taking
care of making sure

00:27:09.770 --> 00:27:12.860
that we can have
availability of networks.

00:27:12.860 --> 00:27:16.030
So the Google networking as
well as the Spanner stack both

00:27:16.030 --> 00:27:21.040
allow us to push the limits of
CAP theorem, as I talked about.

00:27:21.040 --> 00:27:24.580
But yes, as I said,
there's a lot more detail

00:27:24.580 --> 00:27:27.370
in the CAP theorem
and Spanner paper

00:27:27.370 --> 00:27:30.850
that I encourage
you guys to read.

00:27:30.850 --> 00:27:33.430
Next question, how
well does it perform

00:27:33.430 --> 00:27:36.990
over large physical geographies?

00:27:36.990 --> 00:27:41.080
Well is a very interesting term.

00:27:41.080 --> 00:27:47.250
So you can do ACID-consistent
transactions across rows,

00:27:47.250 --> 00:27:50.260
across regions,
across datacenters,

00:27:50.260 --> 00:27:53.560
across continents with Spanner.

00:27:53.560 --> 00:27:55.450
And you can do them
with low latency

00:27:55.450 --> 00:27:58.390
so that you can run
mission-critical applications

00:27:58.390 --> 00:27:59.350
on Spanner.

00:27:59.350 --> 00:28:03.070
And some of it has to do with
the fact that, as I mentioned,

00:28:03.070 --> 00:28:04.540
the Google networking
is fabulous.

00:28:04.540 --> 00:28:09.790
And we can-- the
Cloud Spanner calls--

00:28:09.790 --> 00:28:14.710
basically, the
Cloud Spanner client

00:28:14.710 --> 00:28:18.040
puts any incoming request
onto the Google network

00:28:18.040 --> 00:28:20.350
as quickly as
possible so that it

00:28:20.350 --> 00:28:24.280
can be routed to the
correct Cloud Spanner zone

00:28:24.280 --> 00:28:25.510
to be served.

00:28:25.510 --> 00:28:28.390
And there is
intelligence built in so

00:28:28.390 --> 00:28:32.440
that we can choose
the closest replica

00:28:32.440 --> 00:28:36.910
or get the fastest response.

00:28:36.910 --> 00:28:39.300
So in terms of--

00:28:39.300 --> 00:28:42.250
performance I think is where
you're getting to here.

00:28:42.250 --> 00:28:44.611
And so let me talk
about some of that.

00:28:44.611 --> 00:28:46.360
So basically, in the
multi-region instance

00:28:46.360 --> 00:28:49.620
that we talked about earlier
that I showed a slide on,

00:28:49.620 --> 00:28:56.320
where we have US quorum and read
replicas in Europe and Asia,

00:28:56.320 --> 00:28:58.880
the way to think about it is
that for a multi-region Cloud

00:28:58.880 --> 00:29:00.760
Spanner instance,
you're trading off

00:29:00.760 --> 00:29:03.620
write latency for read latency.

00:29:03.620 --> 00:29:06.220
So you want to have
fast reads everywhere.

00:29:06.220 --> 00:29:09.280
And you can tolerate a little
bit of slowness for writes

00:29:09.280 --> 00:29:10.850
in a particular region.

00:29:10.850 --> 00:29:11.950
So what that means--

00:29:11.950 --> 00:29:16.420
but by slowness, I'm not talking
about seconds and minutes

00:29:16.420 --> 00:29:17.620
and none of that.

00:29:17.620 --> 00:29:19.550
What I'm talking about is--

00:29:19.550 --> 00:29:23.440
for example, if you have a write
coming in from Asia in this

00:29:23.440 --> 00:29:27.700
multi-region configuration,
then we expect because of speed

00:29:27.700 --> 00:29:29.890
of light, which we
haven't solved yet--

00:29:29.890 --> 00:29:34.420
or we don't go faster than
the speed of light yet.

00:29:34.420 --> 00:29:36.640
A call from Asia will
take 300 milliseconds

00:29:36.640 --> 00:29:38.740
round-trip time, 350 maybe.

00:29:38.740 --> 00:29:40.660
So there is that
round-trip time that you

00:29:40.660 --> 00:29:42.790
have to take into
account for latency

00:29:42.790 --> 00:29:47.140
over write that comes in
from Asia for US quorum.

00:29:47.140 --> 00:29:50.394
However, if you want
to do a read from Asia,

00:29:50.394 --> 00:29:52.060
the Cloud Spanner
client is smart enough

00:29:52.060 --> 00:29:54.610
to go to that replica
that's closest

00:29:54.610 --> 00:29:57.880
to it in Asia for the
type of read that you use

00:29:57.880 --> 00:29:58.880
and give you a response.

00:29:58.880 --> 00:30:02.710
And within a region, we expect
five millisecond response,

00:30:02.710 --> 00:30:05.440
depending on the type
of reads that you have.

00:30:05.440 --> 00:30:07.690
Again, these are all
very hand-wavey numbers

00:30:07.690 --> 00:30:11.020
because it really depends on
the type of read you're doing

00:30:11.020 --> 00:30:13.410
and the amount of data
you're reading or writing.

00:30:13.410 --> 00:30:15.230
Like if you're writing
10 gigs of data,

00:30:15.230 --> 00:30:16.870
then it's going to take time.

00:30:16.870 --> 00:30:21.250
But the processing times
for Spanner itself we

00:30:21.250 --> 00:30:24.220
have tried to optimize
as much as possible.

00:30:24.220 --> 00:30:26.620
And we use, as I said,
again the Google network

00:30:26.620 --> 00:30:28.510
which is highly optimized.

00:30:28.510 --> 00:30:33.190
So RTTs, round-trip
times, are much lower

00:30:33.190 --> 00:30:36.340
than what you would get on
the general internet in terms

00:30:36.340 --> 00:30:37.420
of traffic.

00:30:37.420 --> 00:30:40.740
Hopefully that
answered that question.

00:30:40.740 --> 00:30:43.810
Next question, how is
Cloud Spanner managed apart

00:30:43.810 --> 00:30:46.340
from the Google Console?

00:30:46.340 --> 00:30:48.570
Does it have a Cloud
Shell or gcloud SDK?

00:30:48.570 --> 00:30:50.760
Yes, absolutely, absolutely.

00:30:50.760 --> 00:30:53.730
We offer the UI,
obviously, Cloud Console.

00:30:53.730 --> 00:30:56.730
We have a gcloud SDK
that has Cloud Spanner.

00:30:56.730 --> 00:30:59.230
And all the commands
are there for it.

00:30:59.230 --> 00:31:02.780
And so we have a CLI.

00:31:02.780 --> 00:31:05.360
And of course, we have the API
that you can code to directly

00:31:05.360 --> 00:31:06.140
if you so choose.

00:31:06.140 --> 00:31:07.681
In fact, some of
our customers wanted

00:31:07.681 --> 00:31:10.460
to write libraries apart from
the f languages that we had.

00:31:10.460 --> 00:31:12.770
Or they wanted to
instrument it their own way

00:31:12.770 --> 00:31:14.060
or wanted to use REST.

00:31:14.060 --> 00:31:18.680
So we offer GRPC-based client
libraries that you can use.

00:31:18.680 --> 00:31:21.800
And that'd take care of a bunch
of maintenance tasks and things

00:31:21.800 --> 00:31:25.760
like that, like transaction
retries, for example.

00:31:25.760 --> 00:31:30.290
And we offer a REST API as
well so that you can use it.

00:31:30.290 --> 00:31:33.230
If you have applications
that must use REST,

00:31:33.230 --> 00:31:36.650
then you can code directly or
write your own client library

00:31:36.650 --> 00:31:38.640
for the REST command as well.

00:31:38.640 --> 00:31:42.800
And all of this is, by the way,
available on our documentation

00:31:42.800 --> 00:31:43.820
on our site.

00:31:43.820 --> 00:31:47.900
So you can, again, go to
cloud.google.com/spanner/docs.

00:31:47.900 --> 00:31:50.210
And you should have a whole
plethora of documentation

00:31:50.210 --> 00:31:50.710
there.

00:31:54.160 --> 00:31:57.090
Next question, is it
recommended to use cache engines

00:31:57.090 --> 00:31:59.140
like Memcached or Redis
with Cloud Spanner?

00:31:59.140 --> 00:32:00.420
Or is it not necessary?

00:32:00.420 --> 00:32:02.850
Great question.

00:32:02.850 --> 00:32:05.284
This is one of those
things that depends.

00:32:05.284 --> 00:32:07.200
There are a lot of
distributed systems answers

00:32:07.200 --> 00:32:09.210
that are it depends.

00:32:09.210 --> 00:32:12.810
So it depends on the kind
of application you have

00:32:12.810 --> 00:32:15.090
and the latencies
that it requires.

00:32:15.090 --> 00:32:19.320
So in most cases, Cloud
Spanner's latencies

00:32:19.320 --> 00:32:21.690
are good enough where
you can directly

00:32:21.690 --> 00:32:24.090
query the database itself.

00:32:24.090 --> 00:32:29.460
And of course, we cache hot
datasets in memory, obviously.

00:32:29.460 --> 00:32:32.610
So a lot of that is taken care
of by Cloud Spanner itself.

00:32:32.610 --> 00:32:39.510
But if you have large datasets
or datasets that are always

00:32:39.510 --> 00:32:42.120
being read, and you really--

00:32:42.120 --> 00:32:45.190
like a gaming thing, if
you have a gaming app,

00:32:45.190 --> 00:32:47.582
and there's a
bunch of users that

00:32:47.582 --> 00:32:49.290
are playing a game
together, and they all

00:32:49.290 --> 00:32:53.310
need to query some game
state that is not really

00:32:53.310 --> 00:32:54.294
changing that often--

00:32:54.294 --> 00:32:56.710
you need to flush it once in
a while back to the back end,

00:32:56.710 --> 00:33:00.870
but mostly it needs to be served
very fast, like submillisecond

00:33:00.870 --> 00:33:03.582
or whatever-- then you can put
a cache in front of your Cloud

00:33:03.582 --> 00:33:04.290
Spanner database.

00:33:04.290 --> 00:33:07.030
It might be Memcached
or Redis-- your choice.

00:33:07.030 --> 00:33:08.320
And then serve it from there.

00:33:08.320 --> 00:33:14.070
And then most of the QPS is
kind of handled by the cache.

00:33:14.070 --> 00:33:16.920
And once in a while, if there is
some state change in the game,

00:33:16.920 --> 00:33:18.660
then you can flush
it back to Spanner

00:33:18.660 --> 00:33:19.860
and then reload the cache.

00:33:19.860 --> 00:33:23.070
And all of that can of course--
because the database itself

00:33:23.070 --> 00:33:25.770
is mission-critical,
is low latency,

00:33:25.770 --> 00:33:29.010
you can expect that the cache
refreshes don't take that long.

00:33:29.010 --> 00:33:33.240
And you can cycle them based
on your QPS cycles and things

00:33:33.240 --> 00:33:34.945
like that.

00:33:34.945 --> 00:33:36.920
I hope that answered that.

00:33:36.920 --> 00:33:39.740
Next question, what
control do I have over

00:33:39.740 --> 00:33:41.120
how the database works?

00:33:44.150 --> 00:33:46.622
So again, this is--

00:33:46.622 --> 00:33:47.830
I can take this so many ways.

00:33:50.500 --> 00:33:52.730
You've set up your
database with your schema.

00:33:52.730 --> 00:33:54.490
So there is that.

00:33:54.490 --> 00:33:57.160
You control how
the schema works.

00:33:57.160 --> 00:33:59.066
In terms of
introspection tooling,

00:33:59.066 --> 00:34:01.690
if that's what you're asking, we
have a few introspection tools

00:34:01.690 --> 00:34:02.890
already.

00:34:02.890 --> 00:34:05.770
And there are a
bunch more that are

00:34:05.770 --> 00:34:08.480
in the works that will be
coming out later this year.

00:34:08.480 --> 00:34:10.480
But the first introspection
tooling that we have

00:34:10.480 --> 00:34:13.717
is our query plan
visualization that we

00:34:13.717 --> 00:34:14.800
have in the Cloud Console.

00:34:14.800 --> 00:34:17.530
So what you can do is
you can run a SQL query.

00:34:17.530 --> 00:34:21.790
You can see how the query is
performing-- whether there

00:34:21.790 --> 00:34:24.280
are table scans that
you didn't expect,

00:34:24.280 --> 00:34:27.130
et cetera, whether it's doing
a back join with another table

00:34:27.130 --> 00:34:28.219
that you didn't expect.

00:34:28.219 --> 00:34:29.635
So you can look
at the query plans

00:34:29.635 --> 00:34:33.554
and figure out whether
your query's performing

00:34:33.554 --> 00:34:34.679
the way you expected it to.

00:34:34.679 --> 00:34:38.080
And you can make
adjustments based on that.

00:34:38.080 --> 00:34:41.460
As I said, there is a bunch of
other tooling that's coming up.

00:34:41.460 --> 00:34:45.989
We have debug tokens that
we will release soon.

00:34:45.989 --> 00:34:50.370
We have a bunch of
things to give you

00:34:50.370 --> 00:34:52.830
introspection into
the database of how

00:34:52.830 --> 00:34:54.449
the queries are performing.

00:34:54.449 --> 00:34:58.440
In terms of the
database itself, we

00:34:58.440 --> 00:35:02.580
offer QPS metrics to see what
the throughput of your database

00:35:02.580 --> 00:35:03.150
looks like.

00:35:03.150 --> 00:35:08.250
We offer how many megabytes
per second it's getting

00:35:08.250 --> 00:35:11.520
and how many QPS it's
getting and responding with.

00:35:11.520 --> 00:35:15.060
We also obviously show
you the CPU utilization

00:35:15.060 --> 00:35:17.610
of your database because
that's one of the key metrics

00:35:17.610 --> 00:35:20.550
that you need in order
to figure out whether you

00:35:20.550 --> 00:35:22.890
want to add more nodes or not.

00:35:22.890 --> 00:35:25.470
And we offer storage metrics.

00:35:25.470 --> 00:35:29.790
So all of these metrics are also
available through Stackdriver.

00:35:29.790 --> 00:35:32.880
And we have a landing
page for Stackdriver

00:35:32.880 --> 00:35:36.310
where you can see all of
these metrics combined.

00:35:36.310 --> 00:35:38.820
And if you want to set alerts
on any of these metrics,

00:35:38.820 --> 00:35:41.835
you can do so and see them
on the Cloud Spanner console.

00:35:44.760 --> 00:35:46.980
So we're trying to offer
you as much of an integrated

00:35:46.980 --> 00:35:48.400
solution as possible.

00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:50.580
Now again, there
are those who've

00:35:50.580 --> 00:35:53.670
been used to tinkering with
every aspect of the database.

00:35:53.670 --> 00:35:56.780
And for them, there
is some control issue

00:35:56.780 --> 00:35:58.030
that you will have to give up.

00:35:58.030 --> 00:35:59.460
Because it is a managed service.

00:35:59.460 --> 00:36:00.960
The whole point is you
don't have to worry

00:36:00.960 --> 00:36:02.168
about how the database works.

00:36:02.168 --> 00:36:05.160
You just have to worry about
whether your database is

00:36:05.160 --> 00:36:07.670
looking like, or performing,
the way you expect

00:36:07.670 --> 00:36:09.930
it to, whether your schema's
performing the way it's

00:36:09.930 --> 00:36:13.360
expected to, whether you need
to optimize your schema or not.

00:36:13.360 --> 00:36:17.460
And the rest of it, you
kind of have to just let

00:36:17.460 --> 00:36:18.314
others deal with it.

00:36:18.314 --> 00:36:19.980
You never have to--
another way to think

00:36:19.980 --> 00:36:22.200
about it is you
never have to wake up

00:36:22.200 --> 00:36:26.640
at 3:00 in the morning from a
pager because a node fell over,

00:36:26.640 --> 00:36:29.100
or you're running out
of storage capacity.

00:36:29.100 --> 00:36:30.940
We auto-scale
storage, for example.

00:36:30.940 --> 00:36:33.140
So you never have
to worry about that.

00:36:33.140 --> 00:36:37.700
And the only thing you
really need to think about

00:36:37.700 --> 00:36:40.250
is, what is your
compute utilization?

00:36:40.250 --> 00:36:42.440
And do you need to
increase compute depending

00:36:42.440 --> 00:36:46.050
on whether your new app
has become really popular,

00:36:46.050 --> 00:36:48.620
so you add nodes-- which by
the way, you go to the console.

00:36:48.620 --> 00:36:50.720
You change a number in a field.

00:36:50.720 --> 00:36:56.480
And voila, within a matter
of seconds, a few, maybe two

00:36:56.480 --> 00:36:58.465
or three seconds,
you get new nodes.

00:36:58.465 --> 00:37:00.840
And the same thing, you can
actually reduce nodes as well

00:37:00.840 --> 00:37:01.923
just by changing a number.

00:37:01.923 --> 00:37:06.694
So the idea is you don't
have to worry about managing

00:37:06.694 --> 00:37:07.860
or maintaining the database.

00:37:07.860 --> 00:37:10.460
The idea is you leave
the worrying to us.

00:37:10.460 --> 00:37:11.450
Leave the pagers to us.

00:37:11.450 --> 00:37:16.910
And you just have a happy,
running app on your database.

00:37:16.910 --> 00:37:24.030
Next, what happens in the
event of a network failure?

00:37:24.030 --> 00:37:26.810
So I think we talked
about this a lot.

00:37:29.370 --> 00:37:31.800
It depends on where
the network failure is.

00:37:31.800 --> 00:37:34.980
If you're talking about network
failure between Cloud Spanner

00:37:34.980 --> 00:37:38.820
zones or regions, then, as I
said, in the regional case,

00:37:38.820 --> 00:37:40.820
you have three zones--

00:37:40.820 --> 00:37:44.120
which means if there's a network
failure from one of the zones

00:37:44.120 --> 00:37:46.970
to the others,
then first of all,

00:37:46.970 --> 00:37:50.270
it's Google's problem to
repair the network, not yours.

00:37:50.270 --> 00:37:51.980
Second of all, the
other two replicas

00:37:51.980 --> 00:37:53.840
are up and running,
as I talked about.

00:37:53.840 --> 00:37:57.800
So they can serve your
data quite happily,

00:37:57.800 --> 00:38:00.179
without worrying about
the third zone being down.

00:38:00.179 --> 00:38:02.720
And by the way, because there
are a bunch of automatic things

00:38:02.720 --> 00:38:06.050
in place, and because we have
a 24-hour [INAUDIBLE] team

00:38:06.050 --> 00:38:08.960
that's managing and maintaining
these Cloud Spanner instances

00:38:08.960 --> 00:38:11.850
and databases within
them, somebody will be--

00:38:11.850 --> 00:38:14.510
I can guarantee you somebody
will be looking at this problem

00:38:14.510 --> 00:38:16.310
and trying to get
the system back

00:38:16.310 --> 00:38:18.797
online as quick-- for
that zone back online.

00:38:18.797 --> 00:38:20.630
Because the rest of the
zones are available.

00:38:20.630 --> 00:38:23.180
And to you, the Cloud Spanner
instance and databases

00:38:23.180 --> 00:38:25.484
within it are available.

00:38:25.484 --> 00:38:26.900
But somebody's
going to look at it

00:38:26.900 --> 00:38:29.300
and try to bring it back online.

00:38:29.300 --> 00:38:31.580
GCP and Google's
philosophy, actually,

00:38:31.580 --> 00:38:37.160
is to do root cause analysis
later, to get the system up

00:38:37.160 --> 00:38:42.340
and running, to fix the problem
first, and then go and do

00:38:42.340 --> 00:38:44.160
root cause analysis later.

00:38:44.160 --> 00:38:45.950
So that's the one thing.

00:38:45.950 --> 00:38:48.987
In the multi-region
case, multiply it out.

00:38:48.987 --> 00:38:50.570
There's so many more
replicas that you

00:38:50.570 --> 00:38:54.620
can serve the data from that
a single network failure--

00:38:54.620 --> 00:38:59.590
a single entire zone
failure actually

00:38:59.590 --> 00:39:02.830
has almost no effect
on you and your ability

00:39:02.830 --> 00:39:06.740
to serve data from
Cloud Spanner.

00:39:06.740 --> 00:39:08.480
Hopefully that answered that.

00:39:08.480 --> 00:39:12.750
Next question, can I dictate
where my data is stored?

00:39:12.750 --> 00:39:20.000
So you can absolutely choose
the Cloud Spanner configuration.

00:39:20.000 --> 00:39:24.350
And we put your database in
the instance in which you've

00:39:24.350 --> 00:39:28.810
chosen it to be, which means
within the configuration

00:39:28.810 --> 00:39:32.440
within which you've chosen
to put your instance.

00:39:32.440 --> 00:39:35.450
So in the regional
case, if you choose

00:39:35.450 --> 00:39:40.600
US central one, or EU west one,
or Asia east one for your Cloud

00:39:40.600 --> 00:39:44.827
Spanner instance, then
your entire database,

00:39:44.827 --> 00:39:46.910
which means all your compute
and all your storage,

00:39:46.910 --> 00:39:48.580
is stored within that region.

00:39:48.580 --> 00:39:51.640
In the multi-region
case, depending again

00:39:51.640 --> 00:39:54.105
on the multi-region
configuration you choose--

00:39:54.105 --> 00:39:55.480
and by the way,
we tell you which

00:39:55.480 --> 00:39:58.030
regions are going to be
used in that multi-region

00:39:58.030 --> 00:39:59.680
configuration.

00:39:59.680 --> 00:40:02.410
So you are aware
of which regions,

00:40:02.410 --> 00:40:04.930
which datacenters
essentially, the data is in,

00:40:04.930 --> 00:40:09.124
which means which country or
geography or city it's in.

00:40:09.124 --> 00:40:10.540
So you have that
level of control.

00:40:10.540 --> 00:40:14.290
But no, you cannot pick-- if
you want to pick exactly which

00:40:14.290 --> 00:40:19.240
zone, exactly which cluster,
your data is in, then no.

00:40:19.240 --> 00:40:23.050
That defeats the point of having
a managed database service.

00:40:23.050 --> 00:40:26.860
Because you then have
to worry about what

00:40:26.860 --> 00:40:28.510
happens if the zone
goes down, or what

00:40:28.510 --> 00:40:31.090
happens if the storage is
not available in that zone.

00:40:31.090 --> 00:40:32.750
So you don't have
to worry about that.

00:40:32.750 --> 00:40:34.030
If a zone goes down,
we move your data,

00:40:34.030 --> 00:40:35.321
let's say, to a different zone.

00:40:35.321 --> 00:40:37.810
What happens if-- if any
of those scenarios happen,

00:40:37.810 --> 00:40:40.540
we take care of that
situation for you.

00:40:40.540 --> 00:40:42.760
And you just talk to
one database endpoint

00:40:42.760 --> 00:40:45.880
and continue to function.

00:40:45.880 --> 00:40:49.410
Next question, how
do I migrate from

00:40:49.410 --> 00:40:51.990
other relational databases?

00:40:51.990 --> 00:40:53.650
Great question.

00:40:53.650 --> 00:40:57.120
So right now, as I said, if
you have a simple schema,

00:40:57.120 --> 00:41:00.840
it should be fairly simple
to take that schema,

00:41:00.840 --> 00:41:05.290
and translate it into a
Cloud Spanner DDL language

00:41:05.290 --> 00:41:11.100
and into a Cloud Spanner schema,
and then write data into it.

00:41:11.100 --> 00:41:12.940
I know it's a huge
and manual process.

00:41:12.940 --> 00:41:14.190
And I totally understand that.

00:41:14.190 --> 00:41:17.430
We are working on various
ways for our customers

00:41:17.430 --> 00:41:21.330
to make this easier for them.

00:41:21.330 --> 00:41:23.730
The first thing we're working
on is an import export

00:41:23.730 --> 00:41:27.690
functionality that should be
around in the next quarter

00:41:27.690 --> 00:41:34.740
or so that allows you to
take files in a GCS bucket

00:41:34.740 --> 00:41:36.270
and import them into Spanner.

00:41:36.270 --> 00:41:37.920
Like Avro files, for
example, that you

00:41:37.920 --> 00:41:41.070
can take a dump of your
database, put them in Avro,

00:41:41.070 --> 00:41:42.870
put them in a GCS
bucket, and then

00:41:42.870 --> 00:41:45.180
import them into Cloud Spanner.

00:41:45.180 --> 00:41:46.500
You can specify the schema.

00:41:46.500 --> 00:41:48.670
And then at least the
data migration piece

00:41:48.670 --> 00:41:50.820
is taken care of for you.

00:41:50.820 --> 00:41:54.360
Over time, we also are working
on simple, push-button tooling

00:41:54.360 --> 00:41:57.240
so that especially for, as
I said, simple schemas that

00:41:57.240 --> 00:42:00.990
don't require too much
conflict resolution,

00:42:00.990 --> 00:42:03.450
you can just do a
simple migration

00:42:03.450 --> 00:42:05.550
path into Cloud Spanner.

00:42:05.550 --> 00:42:07.800
So we are definitely
thinking about that

00:42:07.800 --> 00:42:10.770
and wanting to make
this an easy solution

00:42:10.770 --> 00:42:12.050
for all of our customers.

00:42:12.050 --> 00:42:14.790
So please hang in there.

00:42:14.790 --> 00:42:16.890
And our last question--

00:42:16.890 --> 00:42:18.810
wow, we've run out of--

00:42:18.810 --> 00:42:23.110
last question, what makes
Cloud Spanner mission critical?

00:42:23.110 --> 00:42:25.140
So great question.

00:42:27.810 --> 00:42:30.000
The point of a
mission-critical database

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:32.160
is that it's highly available.

00:42:32.160 --> 00:42:38.490
It's performant and allows you
to focus on delivering value

00:42:38.490 --> 00:42:42.030
to your customers without
having to worry about database

00:42:42.030 --> 00:42:46.980
management, maintenance,
availability, replication,

00:42:46.980 --> 00:42:50.670
and brittle application
code that will constantly

00:42:50.670 --> 00:42:54.000
break because the strong
consistency and all those kinds

00:42:54.000 --> 00:42:55.400
of semantics are not being met.

00:42:55.400 --> 00:42:57.750
They aren't ACID
transactions, et cetera.

00:42:57.750 --> 00:43:01.080
So Cloud Spanner gives
you-- and we've talked

00:43:01.080 --> 00:43:02.450
about a bunch of these things.

00:43:02.450 --> 00:43:06.370
Cloud Spanner gives you high
performance, high availability,

00:43:06.370 --> 00:43:08.790
built-in replication,
built-in security so that you

00:43:08.790 --> 00:43:12.900
can run your absolutely
business-critical applications

00:43:12.900 --> 00:43:13.650
on Spanner.

00:43:13.650 --> 00:43:15.900
Business critical is
banks, and health care,

00:43:15.900 --> 00:43:19.950
and gaming, and all of
these various scenarios

00:43:19.950 --> 00:43:22.740
in which your business
depends on your data.

00:43:22.740 --> 00:43:24.750
We take that very seriously.

00:43:24.750 --> 00:43:27.690
We've been running Google's
business-critical data

00:43:27.690 --> 00:43:30.210
on Spanner for multiple,
multiple years.

00:43:30.210 --> 00:43:34.970
And we're basically
with that experience

00:43:34.970 --> 00:43:38.000
saying that you can,
with peace of mind,

00:43:38.000 --> 00:43:40.100
put your business-critical
data on Spanner.

00:43:40.100 --> 00:43:43.610
And then you don't have to
worry about the database,

00:43:43.610 --> 00:43:45.370
its availability,
and the ability

00:43:45.370 --> 00:43:47.510
to use it across multiple
regions, geographies,

00:43:47.510 --> 00:43:49.690
and continents.

00:43:49.690 --> 00:43:52.700
So with that, I think
we're out of time.

00:43:52.700 --> 00:43:54.790
If there are anymore
questions, I'm

00:43:54.790 --> 00:43:57.340
happy to answer them
on the platform.

00:43:57.340 --> 00:43:59.372
But I hope you
enjoyed this session.

00:43:59.372 --> 00:44:01.330
And I hope some of the
questions were answered.

00:44:01.330 --> 00:44:03.250
I have to say I
wasn't stumped so far.

00:44:03.250 --> 00:44:05.150
But they were really
good questions.

00:44:05.150 --> 00:44:06.840
So maybe we'll do this again.

00:44:06.840 --> 00:44:10.300
But stay tuned in the
meantime for the next session,

00:44:10.300 --> 00:44:14.470
coming to you live from Google
New York office in a bit.

00:44:14.470 --> 00:44:16.260
Thank you.

